Experiment 3: File Copy using open(), read() & write()
Course Outcome:  CO1
Aim
To write a C program that uses the I/O system calls open(), read() and write() to copy the contents of one file to another.
Algorithm / Theory
●	open() returns a file descriptor used by subsequent I/O system calls; flags such as O_RDONLY, O_WRONLY, O_CREAT and O_TRUNC control how the file is opened.
●	read() transfers bytes from a file into a buffer; write() transfers bytes from a buffer into a file.
●	Unlike the buffered stdio functions (fopen/fread), these are low-level, unbuffered system calls that talk to the kernel directly.
Program
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>
 
#define BUF_SIZE 1024
 
int main() {
    int src_fd, dest_fd;
    ssize_t bytes_read;
    char buffer[BUF_SIZE];
    char source[100], destination[100];
 
    printf("Enter source file name: ");
    scanf("%s", source);
    printf("Enter destination file name: ");
    scanf("%s", destination);
 
    src_fd = open(source, O_RDONLY);
    if (src_fd == -1) {
        perror("Error opening source file");
        exit(1);
    }
 
    dest_fd = open(destination, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, 0644);
    if (dest_fd == -1) {
        perror("Error opening destination file");
        close(src_fd);
        exit(1);
    }
 
    while ((bytes_read = read(src_fd, buffer, BUF_SIZE)) > 0) {
        if (write(dest_fd, buffer, bytes_read) != bytes_read) {
            perror("Error writing to destination file");
            close(src_fd);
            close(dest_fd);
            exit(1);
        }
    }
 
    printf("File copied successfully.\n");
    close(src_fd);
    close(dest_fd);
    return 0;
}
Experiment 4: Multithreaded Program using pthreads
Course Outcome:  CO1
Aim
To write a C program to implement a multithreaded program using POSIX threads (pthreads).
Algorithm / Theory
●	A thread is the smallest unit of CPU execution within a process; multiple threads in the same process share its address space.
●	pthread_create() spawns a new thread that begins executing a given function.
●	pthread_join() blocks the calling thread until the specified thread terminates, similar to how wait() works for processes.
Program
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>
 
void *print_message(void *arg) {
    char *message = (char *) arg;
    int i;
    for (i = 0; i < 3; i++)
        printf("Thread [%s]: iteration %d\n", message, i + 1);
    pthread_exit(NULL);
}
 
int main() {
    pthread_t t1, t2;
 
    pthread_create(&t1, NULL, print_message, (void *) "Thread-1");
    pthread_create(&t2, NULL, print_message, (void *) "Thread-2");
 
    pthread_join(t1, NULL);
    pthread_join(t2, NULL);
 
    printf("Both threads have finished execution.\n");
    return 0;
}

